  "course_description": "Advanced seminar with emphasis on critical texts, methods, and techniques of art historical investigation. Permission from instructor required.",
  "course_notes": "Ethics, Aesthetics, and Politics of the Built Environment What should we do with Nazi buildings? Is OMA¿s CCTV Tower in Beijing the promotion of a propaganda machine, or simply a great work of architecture? Can public buildings like a train station be made to look beautiful, or is that a waste of taxpayer dollars better placed in social programs? Are users complicit when knowingly using a structure constructed through modern forms of slave labor? Where do we draw the line between an architect¿s duty towards a client and towards oneself? This course challenges you to confront contemporary problems in the built environment, at the intersection of ethics, aesthetics, and politics. Studying real-life case-studies, and playing out complex thought experiments, we will debate the multifaceted issues in the spaces we inhabit. Art History major/minor: Division III Architectural Studies minor: elective",
